Q: What is prototypal inheritance?

answer: Prototypal inheritance is the way JavaScript objects share properties and methods with each other. Every object has a hidden link to
another object called its prototype. 
When you access a property that the object doesn’t have itself, JavaScript follows that link and looks in the prototype, then the 
prototype’s prototype, and so on until it finds the property or reaches null. This series of links is called the prototype chain.

code: Creating a prototype with Object.create().

const animal = {
  eats: true,
  eat() { return "Eating..."; }
};

const dog = Object.create(animal);   // dog's prototype is animal
dog.barks = true;

dog.barks;     // true (own property)
dog.eats;      // true (borrowed from animal)
dog.eat(); 

Accessing function's prototype using function.prototype:

function Person(name) {
  this.name = name;               // each instance has its own name
}

Person.prototype.greet = function () {   // shared by all instances
  return "Hi, I'm " + this.name;
};

const a = new Person("Asha");
const b = new Person("Ravi");

a.greet();                               // "Hi, I'm Asha"
a.greet === b.greet;                     // true (same shared function)
Object.getPrototypeOf(a) === Person.prototype;   // true

key points: Every object has an internal prototype link (Object.prototype), accessed with Object.getPrototypeOf(obj).
.prototype is a property on constructor functions and classes. It becomes the prototype of objects created with new. So not accessible to 
objects or arrow functions

Q: What is compose?

answer: Compose is a higher order function (since takes in a function an returns a function). 
It combines several functions into one. The functions run right to left, and each function’s output becomes the next function’s input.

code:

function compose(...functions) {
    return function (initialValue) {
        return functions.reduceRight((acc, fn) => fn(acc), initialValue);
    };
}

const add1 = x => x + 1;
const double = x => x * 2;
const square = x => x * x;

const calc = compose(square, double, add1);
calc(3);

key points: Opposite of compose is pipe which uses reduce to run functions from left to right.

Q: Why does .prototype exist on constructors but not on instances?

answer:.prototype is only present on constructor functions not on instances.
why?
Because we need .prototype on constructor functions so that when we call new Dog() we have some way to set the prototype of the new object 
created. Since we cannot use Object.getPrototype() then as the object is not created yet.
Benefit: Common functions can be added there as well so that memory can be saved while creating instances
But for instances since they dont act as constructor objects they dont need .prototype
